<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Democratic Republic of the Congo</h1><p class="st-meta">1960 CE – present · capital Kinshasa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A large Central African republic, independent from Belgium in 1960, which was Zaire under Mobutu and was the main ground of the Congo wars from 1996.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/belgian-congo">Belgian Congo</a> became independent on 30 June 1960 as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republic-of-the-congo">Republic of the Congo</a>, after the Congolese National Movement led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patrice-lumumba">Patrice Lumumba</a> won the elections in May. Lumumba became prime minister and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-kasavubu">Joseph Kasa-Vubu</a> president. Within weeks the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/force-publique">Force Publique</a> mutinied and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/katanga">Katanga</a> and South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kasai">Kasai</a> broke away.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Kasa-Vubu dismissed Lumumba on 5 September 1960, and Colonel Joseph Mobutu removed him from office on 14 September. Lumumba was executed by Belgian-led Katangan troops on 17 January 1961. Mobutu seized power in a coup in 1965, set up a one-party system and renamed the country the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaire">Zaire</a> in 1971. His government was marked by severe human rights violations, political repression and corruption. In 1997 Laurent-Désiré Kabila's forces overthrew him, and the country took back the name Democratic Republic of the Congo.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-congo-war">Second Congo War</a> began in 1998; the most widely reported estimate of its death toll is 5.4 million people, though other estimates put it at 3 million. Félix Tshisekedi was sworn in as president on 24 January 2019 and reelected in December 2023. The M23 Movement captured Goma and Bukavu in early 2025, and a peace deal between Rwanda and the DRC was signed on 27 June 2025.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/democratic-republic-of-the-congo.jpg" alt="Democratic Republic of the Congo" width="330" height="248"><figcaption>Image: Nightstallion · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Democratic_Republic_of_the_Congo.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1960 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kinshasa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Joseph Kasa-Vubu, Joseph Mobutu, Laurent-Désiré Kabila, Joseph Kabila, Félix Tshisekedi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity (Catholic, Protestant, Kimbanguism), Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French, Kituba, Lingala, Tshiluba, Swahili</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Zaire, Congo-Kinshasa, Republic of the Congo (Léopoldville)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1993&amp;focus=democratic-republic-of-the-congo" aria-label="Democratic Republic of the Congo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5105 2339 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Democratic Republic of the Congo lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5425 2595L5362 2652"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5362 2652h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5425 2595h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5425 2595h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kinshasa. <a href="/atlas?year=1993&amp;focus=democratic-republic-of-the-congo">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
